Open work
=========

Thirteen entries, and every one of them is still open: four bugs, one documented timing choice,
three performance items, and five records of work that was checked and dropped. All were
re-verified against `target/release/small-lisp` at `911040f`, not carried over from an earlier
note, and the source line numbers were corrected in the same pass because earlier commits had
moved them. Provenance for the original finding is the `(Drift.md Xn)` tag; `InProgress/Drift.md`
is the audit those tags point into and is kept as a record of what was found, not as a work queue.
Finished work and its reasoning are in the commit log, not here.

Bug
===

- Deep nesting overflows the native stack with no diagnostic. `MAX_CALL_DEPTH` (2048) bounds
  *call* nesting, but the parser, `copy` and the value renderers recurse natively on *data*
  nesting, which nothing counts. The interpreter thread is given a 256 MB stack
  (`INTERPRETER_STACK`, `src/main.rs:183`), so deep data is survivable until it is not.
  Verified: 100 000 nested arrays or nested parens run fine, 500 000 of either dies with `fatal
  runtime error: stack overflow` and exit -6 (SIGABRT) — no position, no trace, no
  `RecursionError`. Because the release profile is `panic = "abort"`, this is a hard crash,
  which is the exact failure the call-depth guard was added to remove; it just survives on the
  other axis. Fix direction: a nesting counter in the parser with a `RecursionError` shaped like
  the call guard's, plus one in `copy`/render, with the threshold calibrated against the 256 MB
  stack rather than guessed.

- `r+`/`w+` writes land at the reader's prefetch offset, not the read position.
  `src/modules/io.rs:266` does `stream.reader.get_mut().write_all(...)` with no `seek`, and the
  `BufReader` has already pulled the OS cursor forward. Verified: the write lands on the
  `BufReader` fill boundary. With `aaa\nbbb\n`, one `io.read` logically consumes 4 bytes and a
  following write of `ZZZ` lands at offset 8. With a 9005-byte file (`aaa\n` then 9 KB) it lands
  at offset 8192, which is `BufReader`'s default capacity — an implementation detail of the
  buffer, not a position the program ever asked for. `src/tests/io.rs:164-182` passes only because
  its fixture is the single line `initial`, 7 bytes, where the prefetch offset and the
  read position coincide at end of file; a two-line fixture fails. Fix options: (a) drop
  the `BufReader` for writable handles and use one `File` with an explicit `seek` per
  operation; (b) track a logical position beside the buffer and seek before each write.
  (a) is simpler and leaves read-only buffering alone. (Drift.md G4, A4, C1)

- A missing bracket before a `[` selector is silently read as another argument. `postfix_tail`
  (`src/main.rs:779-829`) requires strict adjacency, `token.span.start == e.span.end` at lines
  785 and 807, and gives no diagnostic when the guard fails — the bracketed form simply becomes
  the next argument. Verified: given `(fn (n) n)`, `(g 7[1])` is `TypeError: indexing requires an
  array` because adjacency is honoured, but `(g 7 [1])` is `ArityError: function expects 1, got
  2`, which points at the arity and never at the space. The `.` selector does not fail the same
  way, which is why this reads as a `[`-only bug: `s. k` works, and a detached `s .k` is
  `ParseError: unexpected token Dot`. This is the most likely typo in the language and it
  produces a baffling message. Fix: detect the near-miss in the postfix loop and raise a parse
  error naming the position and suggesting the adjacent form. (Drift.md G7)

- An `expect` failure can be uninformative, because the debug form it is required to use cannot
  show identity. This is not a code defect: spec_en.md §11.6:1189-1190 requires both values to
  use the debug form of §15.9, and `src/main.rs:2426-2427` does exactly that. The consequence is
  the problem, and it has two faces. §15.9:1640 documents a function's debug form as
  `Function(b, c)`, its parameter list only, so two distinct closures both built from
  `(fn (x) x)` produce `expected Function(x), got Function(x)` — verified. The identity rule is
  honoured and the message still says nothing, which is the one case where the assertion is
  hardest to debug. Separately, a module member is a descriptor struct rather than a bare
  function, so comparing `io.write` against a closure puts the entire spec — documentation
  string, arity, type and return — into the message, verified. The fix is a §15.9 decision
  rather than a code one: give the debug form some identity marker, or narrow what `expect`
  renders. It is deliberately left open, and
  `expect_compares_functions_by_identity` asserts only the pass/fail outcome, not the message,
  so either fix can land without rewriting the test. (Drift.md G6, C5)

Diagnostic timing, a documented choice rather than a defect:

- A Ctrl-C that lands during a blocking read is noticed when the read returns, so the notice
  appears after the user's next Enter. `read_line` (`src/modules/io.rs:230-239`) calls
  `check_interrupted()` before and after the read, and `std` retries `EINTR` internally, so the
  signal cannot be observed before the read returns. Making it immediate needs either a
  poll/select loop on the tty (a dependency or hand-written FFI) or I/O from the `ctrlc` handler
  thread, which `ctrlc` does permit, plus a session-owned terminal handle and coordination against
  the prompt. The user chose the data-loss fix only, and spec_en.md §20.10 and Appendix C state
  the timing, so the behaviour is documented rather than implied. (Drift.md G3 residual)

Performance
===========

- Each `set` rebuilds the immutable snapshot chain at every level it passes, O(children) per
  level, so an array or struct write is linear in the children along the path. A structural-
  sharing persistent vector (a 32-way trie was the shape considered) would make array writes
  sub-linear. The trigger criteria are unchanged and deliberately conservative: re-open this only
  when a *realistic* write-heavy workload — nested struct transforms, large-array updates in
  actual scripts — shows `set` consuming more than roughly 30-40% of runtime. The microbenchmark
  alone is not sufficient justification, even though `set_elem_big` is already 4.2x faster than
  before Model P. The `Rc<Vec>` model stays until then: simple, correct, and well tested.

- Module-call spec validation is still re-read on every call. An (arity, type) identity cache
  would remove the remaining scans, but it would go stale if `set` mutates a spec descriptor.
  That belongs with the persistent-representation item above rather than landing alone.

- Tail calls. A call in tail position — in a function body, or in the tail of an `if`, `match`
  or `block` — would run in constant stack, making tail recursion unbounded and widening the
  usable recursion headroom well past `MAX_CALL_DEPTH` (2048). This is no longer a crash
  hazard; the depth guard removed that, so it is a performance and expressiveness item.

Considered and dropped
======================

Recorded so the next reader knows these were re-checked rather than overlooked.

- Drift.md F1-F4 are fixed: the REPL prompt assertions, the interrupt test that drove the wrong
  branch, the `/dev/tty` routing extracted as `ReplSink`, and the doubled-prefix test.
- Drift.md F5-F10 and F12 are now covered, by fourteen tests added in one pass. F12 turned out
  to be stale rather than open: `format_errors_point_at_the_dollar_call` already asserts a
  rendered caret for a `$` error. The rest were genuinely untested, and the audit understated
  two of them. `expect` had *no* test at all, not just no arity test — `ExpectationError` had
  zero occurrences in the suite — so its success path, its two failure-message shapes from
  spec_en.md:1185-1187, its arity error, its string-only third argument and its function
  identity rule are all now asserted. And the width gap was wider than the width: while
  verifying it, three more documented claims turned out to have no test, namely that only `b`
  and `h` accept a width, that `%o` of a negative integer is its full 64-bit two's
  complement, and that reading a reserved or builtin name as a value is a `NameError`
  (spec_en.md:431-432). Each new test asserts the exact documented text rather than the
  current output, so the spec is what is pinned.
- Drift.md F11 is covered and was no longer accurate: `bit-shl` overflow is asserted by name in
  `integer_builtins_turn_operator_overflows_into_lisp_errors` (`src/tests/arithmetic.rs:25`),
  and `(bit-shl 4611686018427387904 2)` is verified as `IntegerOverflow`.
- Drift.md's A and B classes are set aside, not moot. `Archives/spec.txt` still exists and is the
  document `spec_en.md` is translated from, with Appendix C there to track the differences, so an
  A or B finding does have a second document to be measured against. What retired the classes is
  the decision that `spec_en.md` alone is the source of truth: a divergence from `spec.txt` is
  recorded in Appendix C rather than fixed in place. The one A-class case that had a live code
  consequence, A2, was fixed directly when `-NaN` became a literal.
  `README.md` is now a 12-line stub whose only content is a pointer to `spec_en.md`, so the seven
  C findings and four D findings no longer describe any text that exists anywhere in the repo.
- Every `Resolved:` entry that was in this file. 499 lines of resolved history, most of it
  predating the spec rewrite, was the noise that prompted this cleanup. The reasoning lives in
  the commit log; what remains here is what is still true and still undone.
